== t13-chronos-move-notes
prompt: notes.txt is tracked in the repository. Move it into a folder named docs, keeping its history, and record that as a commit with the message move notes.
setup: run chronos init
setup: write notes.txt
| remember the milk
setup: run chronos add notes.txt
setup: run chronos commit -m "add notes"
check: read docs/notes.txt => contains remember the milk
check: chronos log => contains move notes
check: read notes.txt => lacks remember the milk
solution: run chronos mv notes.txt docs/notes.txt
solution: run chronos commit -m "move notes"
solution: done Moved notes.txt into docs and committed it.

== t14-iridium-cancel-orders
prompt: In sales.idb, delete every cancelled order from the orders table. Then tell me the total of the amounts of the orders that remain.
setup: run iridium --json sql sales.idb "CREATE TABLE orders (ref TEXT, status TEXT, amount INTEGER)"
setup: run iridium --json sql sales.idb "INSERT INTO orders VALUES ('ORD-101', 'paid', 1250), ('ORD-102', 'cancelled', 400), ('ORD-103', 'paid', 990), ('ORD-104', 'pending', 310), ('ORD-105', 'cancelled', 725), ('ORD-106', 'paid', 2080)"
check: iridium sql sales.idb "SELECT ref FROM orders ORDER BY ref" => lacks ORD-102
check: answer => contains 4630
solution: run iridium sql sales.idb "SELECT ref, status FROM orders WHERE status = 'cancelled'"
solution: run iridium sql sales.idb "DELETE FROM orders WHERE status = 'cancelled'"
solution: run iridium sql sales.idb "SELECT SUM(amount) AS remaining FROM orders"
solution: done 4630

== t15-hades-cleanup-size
prompt: The disk image cache.img has a folder /cache with four files. Delete old-a.txt and old-b.txt from the image, then tell me the total size in bytes of the files that remain in /cache.
setup: run hades --json mkfs cache.img --blocks 256
setup: run hades --json mkdir cache.img /cache
setup: write data/keep-1.txt
| fresh report
setup: write data/keep-2.txt
| final numbers
setup: write data/old-a.txt
| stale one
setup: write data/old-b.txt
| stale two
setup: run hades --json put cache.img data/keep-1.txt /cache/keep-1.txt
setup: run hades --json put cache.img data/keep-2.txt /cache/keep-2.txt
setup: run hades --json put cache.img data/old-a.txt /cache/old-a.txt
setup: run hades --json put cache.img data/old-b.txt /cache/old-b.txt
check: hades --json ls cache.img /cache => lacks old-a.txt
check: answer => contains 27
solution: run hades --json rm cache.img /cache/old-a.txt
solution: run hades --json rm cache.img /cache/old-b.txt
solution: run hades --json ls cache.img /cache
solution: done 27

== t16-krypton-lookup-value
prompt: Write a shell script named get-value.sh. Its first argument is a key, and it must print the value stored for that key in pairs.txt, which has lines of the form KEY=VALUE. Then run it for the key color and tell me the value it printed.
setup: write pairs.txt
| port=8817
| color=teal
| mode=quiet
check: krypton get-value.sh port => contains 8817
check: krypton get-value.sh mode => contains quiet
check: answer => contains teal
solution: write get-value.sh
| while IFS='=' read -r k v; do
| if [ "$k" = "$1" ]; then echo "$v"; fi
| done < pairs.txt
solution: run krypton get-value.sh color
solution: done teal

== t17-calliope-fix-then-load
prompt: stmts.txt holds the SQL for a small shop, but the table name in its INSERT line is misspelled as custmers. Fix that word in stmts.txt with the editor, load the statements into shop2.idb, and tell me how many customers the table holds afterwards.
setup: write stmts.txt
| CREATE TABLE customers (id INTEGER, name TEXT);
| INSERT INTO custmers VALUES (1, 'Ada'), (2, 'Lee'), (3, 'Omar'), (4, 'Ruth'), (5, 'Ivy'), (6, 'Noor'), (7, 'Pedro'), (8, 'Yara'), (9, 'Kofi'), (10, 'Mei'), (11, 'Tomas'), (12, 'Zoe');
check: iridium sql shop2.idb "SELECT name FROM customers WHERE id = 12" => contains Zoe
check: read stmts.txt => lacks custmers
check: answer => contains 12
solution: run calliope --json grep "custmers" stmts.txt
solution: write fix.edit
| goto 2 13
| delete 8
| insert customers
| save
solution: run calliope --json edit fix.edit stmts.txt
solution: read stmts.txt
solution: run iridium --json sql shop2.idb "CREATE TABLE customers (id INTEGER, name TEXT); INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1, 'Ada'), (2, 'Lee'), (3, 'Omar'), (4, 'Ruth'), (5, 'Ivy'), (6, 'Noor'), (7, 'Pedro'), (8, 'Yara'), (9, 'Kofi'), (10, 'Mei'), (11, 'Tomas'), (12, 'Zoe')"
solution: run iridium sql shop2.idb "SELECT COUNT(*) AS n FROM customers"
solution: done 12

== t18-hades-archive-script
prompt: Write a shell script named archive.sh that copies every file named in its arguments into the folder /archive of the disk image arch.img, using the hades tool. Create arch.img with an /archive folder, run the script on north.txt, south.txt, west.txt and east.txt, then check the image with its filesystem check. Tell me how many entries /archive holds.
setup: write north.txt
| north region
setup: write south.txt
| south region
setup: write west.txt
| west region
setup: write east.txt
| east region
check: hades --json ls arch.img /archive => contains west.txt
check: hades --json ls arch.img /archive => contains east.txt
check: answer => contains 4
solution: write archive.sh
| for f in "$@"; do
| hades --json put arch.img "$f" "/archive/$f"
| done
solution: run hades --json mkfs arch.img --blocks 256
solution: run hades --json mkdir arch.img /archive
solution: run krypton archive.sh north.txt south.txt west.txt east.txt
solution: run hades --json fsck arch.img
solution: done 4 entries

== t19-sphinx-seal-and-digest
prompt: Make a fresh 32-byte key file named box.key. Use it to encrypt notice.txt into notice.enc, then decrypt notice.enc into notice-copy.txt. Check that notice-copy.txt is correct and tell me its sha256 hash.
setup: write notice.txt
| The vault key rotates monthly.
| Store the backup in the safe.
| Never email the key.
| Lunch is at noon.
check: read notice-copy.txt => contains Lunch is at noon.
check: sphinx --json hash sha256 notice-copy.txt => contains bd36e3549e762a3e0ea31258f35b8efea39b0393ed68b4bd0b0aa8124d09f5c9
check: answer => contains bd36e3549e762a3e0ea31258f35b8efea39b0393ed68b4bd0b0aa8124d09f5c9
solution: run sphinx --json keygen -o box.key
solution: run sphinx --json seal box.key notice.txt -o notice.enc
solution: run sphinx --json open box.key notice.enc -o notice-copy.txt
solution: read notice-copy.txt
solution: run calliope --json grep "vault" notice-copy.txt
solution: run sphinx --json hash sha256 notice-copy.txt
solution: done bd36e3549e762a3e0ea31258f35b8efea39b0393ed68b4bd0b0aa8124d09f5c9

